      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "A polymorphism of GLP-1 R (rs6923761) has potential implications in weight loss and metabolic control. We decide to investigate the role of this polymorphism on metabolic changes and weight loss secondary to treatment with liraglutide."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
            "content": "A population of 90 patients with diabetes mellitus type 2 and overweight, unable to achieve glycemic control (HbA1c>7%) with metformin alone that require initiation of liraglutide treatment in progressive dose to 1.8mg/day subcutaneously, was analyzed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Fifty one patients (56.7%) had the genotype GG and 39 (43.3%) patients; GA (30 patients, 33.3%) or AA (9 patients, 10%) (A allele carriers). In patients with both genotypes, body mass index (BMI), weight and fat decreased. The proportion of the mentioned reductions was higher in the variant allele carriers; BMI (-0.59±2.5kg/m(2) vs. -1.69±3.9kg/m(2); P<0.05), weight (-2.78±2.8kg vs. -4.52±4.6kg; P<0.05) and fat mass (-0.59±2.5kg vs. -1.69±3.9kg; P<0.05). Weight reduction after liraglutide treatment was greater in the A-allele carriers by 2.9kg (95% CI: 0.27-5.64). The decrease of basal glucose, HOMA-R and HbA1c was similar in both genotypes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Our data showed better anthropometric parameters in overweight diabetic subjects with the variant allele (A) of rs6923761 GLP-1 R polymorphism. A allele carriers had a greater decrease in weight and fat mass after treatment with liraglutide. The present study is a preliminary observation, and its results need to be replicated with a higher number of patients in different populations."
          }
        ]
      },
